587
00:31:52,200 --> 00:31:55,120
But I want to kind of hone in on exactly what this means

588
00:31:55,120 --> 00:31:57,520
where we're moving towards a world that's

589
00:31:57,520 --> 00:32:01,840
task-based and one-off kind of request to an AI tool,

590
00:32:01,840 --> 00:32:05,040
and more so a team of bots kind of at your service

591
00:32:05,040 --> 00:32:07,000
working together on a task.

592
00:32:07,000 --> 00:32:10,840
So we're really going from a chat bot type world

593
00:32:10,840 --> 00:32:12,760
to more like a team of colleagues.

594
00:32:12,760 --> 00:32:15,680
And what's nice about this is when I think about Grok Bot,

595
00:32:15,680 --> 00:32:17,560
I think about a tool that actually gives me

596
00:32:17,560 --> 00:32:18,800
finished work.

597
00:32:18,800 --> 00:32:20,760
And that's the beauty of it is it's not

598
00:32:20,760 --> 00:32:23,480
meant to be something that you have to constantly babysit

599
00:32:23,480 --> 00:32:26,320
or something that is always just at your computer

600
00:32:26,360 --> 00:32:28,960
that you always have to keep your laptop open for.

601
00:32:28,960 --> 00:32:32,600
It's literally meant to be like a teammate where it can work

602
00:32:32,600 --> 00:32:33,880
maybe while you're on vacation.

603
00:32:33,880 --> 00:32:34,440
You're away.

604
00:32:34,440 --> 00:32:35,880
It can work while you're asleep.

605
00:32:35,880 --> 00:32:38,040
It can work because you can talk to it on your phone

606
00:32:38,040 --> 00:32:39,560
like you would a normal colleague.

607
00:32:39,560 --> 00:32:42,800
And so we're going towards a world

608
00:32:42,800 --> 00:32:44,520
where that's going to be happening all the time

609
00:32:44,520 --> 00:32:46,480
for all different types of domains.

610
00:32:46,480 --> 00:32:48,480
So whether you're in sales, you're in product,

611
00:32:48,480 --> 00:32:49,880
you're in engineering, you're going

612
00:32:49,880 --> 00:32:52,520
to have a staff or a team of bots at your service that

613
00:32:52,520 --> 00:32:55,240
can help you basically accomplish work that maybe you

614
00:32:55,240 --> 00:32:57,040
didn't have time for before, maybe you're not

615
00:32:57,040 --> 00:32:58,400
looking forward to.

616
00:32:58,400 --> 00:33:01,440
But you're also having them ready to go

617
00:33:01,440 --> 00:33:05,950
with the context and memory that you're providing them.

618
00:33:05,950 --> 00:33:08,750
OK, so for folks who haven't seen Grok Bot before,

619
00:33:08,750 --> 00:33:10,710
first I recommend downloading it.

620
00:33:10,710 --> 00:33:13,230
It's on Android, iOS, and the Mac.

621
00:33:13,230 --> 00:33:15,670
So if you haven't downloaded it yet, please do.

622
00:33:15,670 --> 00:33:18,070
And it's the best way to see it and try it out

623
00:33:18,070 --> 00:33:19,430
for yourself.

624
00:33:19,430 --> 00:33:21,870
There's a couple main things to highlight here.

625
00:33:21,870 --> 00:33:24,630
The first is the interface itself.

626
00:33:24,630 --> 00:33:28,510
So if you've used iMessage or maybe any form of a chat

627
00:33:28,510 --> 00:33:32,030
or texting interface, you'll see that it looks pretty similar.

628
00:33:32,030 --> 00:33:34,230
On the left is where all your bots live.

629
00:33:34,230 --> 00:33:36,470
And as you can see, you can message them like teammates.

630
00:33:36,470 --> 00:33:39,390
So you can say good morning to your bots, you can thank them.

631
00:33:39,390 --> 00:33:41,110
You can basically go back and forth with them

632
00:33:41,110 --> 00:33:43,750
because they have this long-lived memory

633
00:33:43,750 --> 00:33:46,470
that it's able to learn from what you've said before.

634
00:33:46,470 --> 00:33:47,770
If maybe you've told it that you

635
00:33:47,770 --> 00:33:50,030
didn't like how it did something a particular way,

636
00:33:50,030 --> 00:33:51,530
it keeps that in its memory, and it's

637
00:33:51,530 --> 00:33:53,390
able to do better the next time around.

638
00:33:53,390 --> 00:33:57,270
And we'll see an example of that actually in this demo.

639
00:33:57,270 --> 00:34:00,150
You will also see that bots have their own computers.

640
00:34:00,150 --> 00:34:02,470
And so this is kind of what I mean where bots are like

641
00:34:02,470 --> 00:34:07,390
colleagues, is no longer do you have to maybe connect each bot

642
00:34:07,390 --> 00:34:10,110
to every single service or provider

643
00:34:10,110 --> 00:34:12,070
that you want to have access to.

644
00:34:12,070 --> 00:34:14,710
Your bot can do a lot of that for you.

645
00:34:14,710 --> 00:34:17,710
An example that we did yesterday was a bot making its own Google

646
00:34:17,710 --> 00:34:18,710
form.

647
00:34:18,710 --> 00:34:20,150
An example that I've heard from the crowd

648
00:34:20,150 --> 00:34:23,190
is a bot logging into MongoDB and kind of analyzing

649
00:34:23,310 --> 00:34:25,470
your database and making sure that everything

650
00:34:25,470 --> 00:34:27,230
is up to snuff.

651
00:34:27,230 --> 00:34:29,790
And then I think another cool thing about computer use

652
00:34:29,790 --> 00:34:32,950
is you can monitor what it's doing in the computer itself.

653
00:34:32,950 --> 00:34:35,310
So if, for example, I want to take over its computer,

654
00:34:35,310 --> 00:34:36,510
I totally can.

655
00:34:36,510 --> 00:34:38,710
I can also control from an enterprise level

656
00:34:38,710 --> 00:34:41,270
what it has access to in that computer.

657
00:34:41,270 --> 00:34:42,670
So if for some reason I don't want

658
00:34:42,670 --> 00:34:44,310
it to access a particular website

659
00:34:44,310 --> 00:34:46,390
or I don't want it to download a particular thing,

660
00:34:46,390 --> 00:34:48,670
I can always keep that in my admin controls

661
00:34:48,670 --> 00:34:50,830
so that you don't want the bot making decisions

662
00:34:50,830 --> 00:34:52,950
that you wouldn't take yourself.

663
00:34:52,950 --> 00:34:54,710
And then automations and routines.

664
00:34:54,710 --> 00:34:55,830
That's a huge one.

665
00:34:55,830 --> 00:34:57,830
And I think it's something that people constantly

666
00:34:57,830 --> 00:35:00,910
look for with AI tools is, hey, how can I automate

667
00:35:00,910 --> 00:35:03,350
or have a repetitive task be done without me

668
00:35:03,350 --> 00:35:05,070
having to think about it?

669
00:35:05,070 --> 00:35:06,390
A common one that I have actually

670
00:35:06,390 --> 00:35:09,670
is I subscribe to a bunch of different AI newsletters

671
00:35:09,670 --> 00:35:10,830
and podcasts.

672
00:35:10,830 --> 00:35:13,030
And I've started not to keep track of all of them.

673
00:35:13,030 --> 00:35:15,310
And so every morning, I'll have bot actually

674
00:35:15,310 --> 00:35:17,150
look through my email, find the newsletters that

675
00:35:17,150 --> 00:35:19,310
are relevant to me, and then just create them

676
00:35:19,310 --> 00:35:21,870
in a daily digest and summarize them in Slack.

677
00:35:21,870 --> 00:35:24,030
And then it just messages me every morning saying, hey,

678
00:35:24,030 --> 00:35:27,270
here's what you missed from these podcasts, these newsletters.

679
00:35:27,270 --> 00:35:28,870
And it helps me keep up to date.

680
00:35:28,870 --> 00:35:31,670
But it's also something that I no longer have to think about.

681
00:35:31,670 --> 00:35:34,390
Similarly, there's a very common morning brief, morning

682
00:35:34,390 --> 00:35:36,510
digest for your work if there's Slack messages

683
00:35:36,510 --> 00:35:38,510
that you've missed overnight.

684
00:35:38,510 --> 00:35:40,950
I do like to highlight that for distributed teams,

685
00:35:40,950 --> 00:35:42,630
GrokBot is really a game changer

686
00:35:42,630 --> 00:35:46,550
because you can go from having to wait on work being

687
00:35:46,550 --> 00:35:48,870
done from other teammates or other tools

688
00:35:48,870 --> 00:35:51,590
to just having something that's really always on and always

689
00:35:51,630 --> 00:35:52,430
at your service.

690
00:35:52,430 --> 00:35:54,070
And so that's kind of the beauty of GrokBot

691
00:35:54,070 --> 00:35:56,830
is that it's really great for distributed teams.

692
00:35:56,830 --> 00:35:58,870
But also, it's a multiplier.

693
00:35:58,870 --> 00:36:02,280
It's a force multiplier for you wherever you are.

694
00:36:02,280 --> 00:36:04,560
And then the last thing is sharing your bots with others.

695
00:36:04,560 --> 00:36:06,720
So you see here on these kind of little coasters

696
00:36:06,720 --> 00:36:08,760
that we have QR codes.

697
00:36:08,760 --> 00:36:10,720
That's for public bots that are

698
00:36:10,720 --> 00:36:12,200
available on the marketplace.

699
00:36:12,200 --> 00:36:14,320
If you want to share bots with your team at work,

700
00:36:14,320 --> 00:36:15,720
you can do that as well.

701
00:36:15,720 --> 00:36:18,040
For example, we have bots that are specific to the sales

702
00:36:18,040 --> 00:36:19,640
team that we all share.

703
00:36:19,640 --> 00:36:21,140
There's bots on the engineering team

704
00:36:21,200 --> 00:36:22,700
that they share with us as well.

705
00:36:22,700 --> 00:36:24,300
And so bots are meant to be multiplayer.

706
00:36:24,300 --> 00:36:26,500
They're meant to be used and shared.

707
00:36:26,500 --> 00:36:29,820
And also creates a sense of consistency across the company.

708
00:36:29,820 --> 00:36:33,140
You want to make sure that, let's say, the sales bot is not

709
00:36:33,140 --> 00:36:35,780
using different resources that maybe someone else's sales

710
00:36:35,780 --> 00:36:36,280
bot is.

711
00:36:36,280 --> 00:36:41,100
So having that consistency of bots is also very important.

712
00:36:41,100 --> 00:36:43,380
OK, I touched a little bit on some of this,

713
00:36:43,380 --> 00:36:46,260
like why GrokBot and what makes it different.

714
00:36:46,260 --> 00:36:47,940
I think the main things to highlight here

715
00:36:47,940 --> 00:36:50,020
is it's very easy to use.

716
00:36:50,020 --> 00:36:52,220
I think a lot of tools, when you first spin it up,

717
00:36:52,220 --> 00:36:53,140
you don't know where to start.

718
00:36:53,140 --> 00:36:55,020
You don't know how to connect things.

719
00:36:55,020 --> 00:36:57,620
GrokBot kind of learns all of that for you.

720
00:36:57,620 --> 00:37:00,580
You'll see it actually when we work with a bot from scratch

721
00:37:00,580 --> 00:37:01,780
today.

722
00:37:01,780 --> 00:37:04,980
It will basically ask me, hey, what am I being used for,

723
00:37:04,980 --> 00:37:06,740
and how can I best help you?

724
00:37:06,740 --> 00:37:08,360
And we'll also do something cool.

725
00:37:08,360 --> 00:37:10,900
We'll actually ask one of the GrokBots today

726
00:37:10,900 --> 00:37:12,620
to spin up its own team of bots.

727
00:37:12,620 --> 00:37:15,420
I'm basically going to ask it, hey, what bots do you

728
00:37:15,420 --> 00:37:17,540
think would be helpful for you?

729
00:37:17,540 --> 00:37:20,140
If I had to ask you to hire your own team,

730
00:37:20,140 --> 00:37:21,300
what would be useful for you?

731
00:37:21,300 --> 00:37:23,380
And a bot can actually make that decision.

732
00:37:23,380 --> 00:37:24,940
And so bots spinning up other bots

733
00:37:24,940 --> 00:37:27,300
or bots working with their other teammates

734
00:37:27,300 --> 00:37:30,420
is what really makes GrokBot this multiplayer force

735
00:37:30,420 --> 00:37:32,580
multiplier tool.

736
00:37:32,580 --> 00:37:34,500
And then, as I mentioned, computer use and the fact

737
00:37:34,500 --> 00:37:38,020
that it can do an entire task end to end.

738
00:37:38,020 --> 00:37:39,740
One thing that I really like about GrokBot

739
00:37:39,740 --> 00:37:42,540
is if I give it a task, and again, it's in the cloud,

740
00:37:42,540 --> 00:37:44,820
so it's always on, I can come back and trust

741
00:37:44,820 --> 00:37:46,260
that the task will be done.

742
00:37:46,260 --> 00:37:48,220
It's not going to sit waiting for my approval.

743
00:37:48,220 --> 00:37:49,380
It's not going to go back and forth

744
00:37:49,380 --> 00:37:50,700
and like, should I do this decision?

745
00:37:50,700 --> 00:37:51,460
Should I not?

746
00:37:51,460 --> 00:37:52,880
It will finish the task, and then I

747
00:37:52,880 --> 00:37:55,020
can tweak it or steer it however I want.

748
00:37:55,020 --> 00:37:57,420
But the goal is that you're not just sitting and waiting

749
00:37:57,420 --> 00:37:58,580
for it to do something.

750
00:37:58,580 --> 00:38:01,900
You're actually coming back to finished work, which really,

751
00:38:01,900 --> 00:38:05,160
really saves a lot of time.

752
00:38:05,160 --> 00:38:06,520
Cool.

753
00:38:06,520 --> 00:38:09,360
So as I mentioned, these are some sales engineer use cases

754
00:38:09,360 --> 00:38:13,240
that I have seen at SpaceX, and also three of them,

755
00:38:13,240 --> 00:38:15,640
actually, I'll be showing today.

756
00:38:15,640 --> 00:38:17,920
And so the engineer technical resource,

757
00:38:17,920 --> 00:38:20,200
David Gann will probably go more into detail about this

758
00:38:20,200 --> 00:38:22,160
and his customer support session.

759
00:38:22,160 --> 00:38:25,000
So if you're interested in that, definitely take a look.

760
00:38:25,000 --> 00:38:27,960
But what's nice about this is we get a lot of customer

761
00:38:27,960 --> 00:38:29,600
questions about security.

762
00:38:29,600 --> 00:38:31,880
We get questions about how GrokBot was built,

763
00:38:31,880 --> 00:38:35,320
how is memory being stored, what type of computer

764
00:38:35,320 --> 00:38:37,240
is it using as a Linux VM.

765
00:38:37,240 --> 00:38:39,160
All of these technical questions, some of them

766
00:38:39,160 --> 00:38:40,680
are in our docs, but some of them

767
00:38:40,680 --> 00:38:43,200
also are just in the implementation of GrokBot.

768
00:38:43,200 --> 00:38:44,800
It's in the repository.

769
00:38:44,800 --> 00:38:46,400
And so what I do with the sales engineer

770
00:38:46,400 --> 00:38:48,480
is I actually have a expert.

771
00:38:48,480 --> 00:38:49,840
I call him Sherlock.

772
00:38:49,840 --> 00:38:53,340
And what he does is he basically has access to a repository.

773
00:38:53,340 --> 00:38:56,280
So he has access to the GrokBot repository.

774
00:38:56,280 --> 00:38:59,440
And so if a customer asked me a question, I can go ask Sherlock,

775
00:38:59,440 --> 00:39:03,080
hey, a customer is curious, do we support X, Y, and Z?

776
00:39:03,080 --> 00:39:04,440
Sherlock comes back.

777
00:39:04,440 --> 00:39:06,200
But what's nice about this is I've actually

778
00:39:06,200 --> 00:39:09,240
steered Sherlock to make sure that he doesn't ever

779
00:39:09,240 --> 00:39:12,520
release any IP, that he phrases things in an easy understand

780
00:39:12,520 --> 00:39:14,720
way, if I need to communicate this to maybe

781
00:39:14,720 --> 00:39:17,560
someone more high level, someone that's not down in the weeds.

782
00:39:17,560 --> 00:39:21,040
And so that's kind of the beauty of this particular GrokBot

783
00:39:21,040 --> 00:39:23,840
is that it's meant to understand things technically,

784
00:39:23,840 --> 00:39:26,760
