8825
07:31:38,060 --> 07:31:40,260
So first, what is Grok Bot?

8826
07:31:40,260 --> 07:31:43,860
Grok Bot is a new product that lets you hand off real work

8827
07:31:43,860 --> 07:31:46,020
to an AI coworker.

8828
07:31:46,020 --> 07:31:48,980
The interface looks very familiar,

8829
07:31:48,980 --> 07:31:53,150
just like a messenger app on your phone or a computer.

8830
07:31:53,150 --> 07:31:55,990
But under the hood, it's built to keep working

8831
07:31:55,990 --> 07:31:58,790
after you've sent your original message.

8832
07:31:58,790 --> 07:32:00,590
So you aren't just chatting back and forth

8833
07:32:00,590 --> 07:32:04,190
with quick, simple one-offs here and there.

8834
07:32:04,190 --> 07:32:08,170
You're really able to delegate a real project to Grok Bot,

8835
07:32:08,170 --> 07:32:12,880
and it can go off and take it from there.

8836
07:32:12,880 --> 07:32:14,360
One of my favorite things about Grok Bot

8837
07:32:14,360 --> 07:32:17,280
is how flexible it is to the way you work.

8838
07:32:17,280 --> 07:32:19,880
So you can start with a single general bot

8839
07:32:19,880 --> 07:32:22,560
to help you get set up quickly and easily,

8840
07:32:22,560 --> 07:32:24,480
or create a team of specialists that

8841
07:32:24,480 --> 07:32:27,040
coordinate around the task and work in parallel

8842
07:32:27,040 --> 07:32:30,240
on certain sub-tasks.

8843
07:32:30,240 --> 07:32:32,440
And however your team operates, Grok Bot

8844
07:32:32,440 --> 07:32:37,650
can fit in and be very useful very quickly.

8845
07:32:37,650 --> 07:32:39,930
So why Grok Bot?

8846
07:32:39,930 --> 07:32:42,210
Why does it feel like interacting

8847
07:32:42,210 --> 07:32:47,130
with a capable coworker rather than just another AI tool?

8848
07:32:47,130 --> 07:32:49,680
For me, there are three big reasons.

8849
07:32:49,680 --> 07:32:52,440
First, Grok Bot is always on.

8850
07:32:52,440 --> 07:32:54,680
So by now, you may have heard that Grok Bots

8851
07:32:54,680 --> 07:32:57,820
have their own computer.

8852
07:32:57,820 --> 07:33:00,500
All that really means is that they

8853
07:33:00,500 --> 07:33:02,140
can work without using yours, which

8854
07:33:02,140 --> 07:33:06,360
is a little bit more powerful than you would think.

8855
07:33:06,360 --> 07:33:07,880
So if you close your laptop or you

8856
07:33:07,880 --> 07:33:10,040
are working on something else, your Grok Bots

8857
07:33:10,040 --> 07:33:12,800
can still finish the task you assigned them

8858
07:33:12,800 --> 07:33:15,440
without you having to check in constantly

8859
07:33:15,440 --> 07:33:19,680
and steer them too much or slowing down your local machine.

8860
07:33:19,680 --> 07:33:22,320
There are also routines, which are

8861
07:33:22,320 --> 07:33:24,840
cron jobs or event-based triggers

8862
07:33:24,840 --> 07:33:27,160
that you can launch your Grok Bots with

8863
07:33:27,160 --> 07:33:30,320
and make them super reactive.

8864
07:33:30,320 --> 07:33:33,240
This way, you don't have to be online at the exact moment.

8865
07:33:33,240 --> 07:33:38,040
You want them to run or start working on the task.

8866
07:33:38,040 --> 07:33:41,280
And these are simple, or these are super simple to set up.

8867
07:33:41,280 --> 07:33:44,360
All you have to do is tell it, create a routine at this time

8868
07:33:44,360 --> 07:33:49,030
or on this event, just like a normal conversation,

8869
07:33:49,030 --> 07:33:51,640
and it will set it up for you.

8870
07:33:51,640 --> 07:33:54,560
Second, it is really easy to use.

8871
07:33:54,560 --> 07:33:57,650
The interface is familiar for a reason.

8872
07:33:57,650 --> 07:33:59,090
You don't have to be an engineer that

8873
07:33:59,090 --> 07:34:02,090
is familiar with the ins and outs of an IDE

8874
07:34:02,090 --> 07:34:03,870
to use bots effectively.

8875
07:34:03,870 --> 07:34:05,530
You can just talk with it normally

8876
07:34:05,530 --> 07:34:09,760
like you would a friend or teammate on Slack.

8877
07:34:09,760 --> 07:34:12,120
And finally, it fits how you actually work.

8878
07:34:12,120 --> 07:34:15,160
There are already connectors for most tools you use.

8879
07:34:15,160 --> 07:34:17,920
You just have to go into the marketplace and search for them,

8880
07:34:17,920 --> 07:34:21,520
and there is likely one for you.

8881
07:34:21,520 --> 07:34:24,440
So you can plug Grok Bot immediately

8882
07:34:24,440 --> 07:34:27,720
into your ticketing system, whether that's

8883
07:34:27,720 --> 07:34:36,040
plain, Zendesk, Intercom, where your team chats like Slack,

8884
07:34:36,040 --> 07:34:38,880
where your knowledge base is like Notion.

8885
07:34:38,880 --> 07:34:41,640
And then from there, you can use the bots and routines

8886
07:34:41,640 --> 07:34:45,960
like building blocks that fit the way your team actually works.

8887
07:34:45,960 --> 07:34:49,660
And one really powerful thing about Grok Bot

8888
07:34:49,660 --> 07:34:52,540
is if there is a missing piece, you can likely

8889
07:34:52,540 --> 07:34:56,000
have Grok Bot build it.

8890
07:34:56,000 --> 07:34:57,920
Grok Bot has access to Cloud agents.

8891
07:34:57,920 --> 07:35:00,440
So if there is something that you're missing,

8892
07:35:00,440 --> 07:35:03,260
you can just have it tell it to spin up a Cloud agent

8893
07:35:03,260 --> 07:35:06,120
to build a certain connector or a certain thing,

8894
07:35:06,120 --> 07:35:08,830
and it can go off and do it for you.

8895
07:35:08,830 --> 07:35:10,490
So you can get creative and build around,

8896
07:35:10,490 --> 07:35:14,530
and you're not stuck waiting on N's roadmap or a third party

8897
07:35:14,530 --> 07:35:20,580
vendor to add support for the thing you need.

8898
07:35:20,580 --> 07:35:21,080
Cool.

8899
07:35:21,080 --> 07:35:24,600
Now we'll get into some customer support use cases.

8900
07:35:24,600 --> 07:35:28,600
So the big one is always, can it answer support tickets?

8901
07:35:28,600 --> 07:35:32,580
The answer is yes, and I'll show this later in the demo.

8902
07:35:32,580 --> 07:35:34,420
I know this can be scary at first

8903
07:35:34,420 --> 07:35:38,260
to let an agent reply to your customers.

8904
07:35:38,260 --> 07:35:40,020
So you can definitely build up to it.

8905
07:35:40,020 --> 07:35:43,640
So you can crawl, then walk, then run.

8906
07:35:43,640 --> 07:35:45,740
At first, you can have it just read tickets,

8907
07:35:45,740 --> 07:35:49,700
maybe give you a summary and the root issue of what

8908
07:35:49,700 --> 07:35:52,700
the user is asking about.

8909
07:35:52,700 --> 07:35:55,420
And maybe it can draft a response.

8910
07:35:55,420 --> 07:35:57,980
Then you can have it add the draft

8911
07:35:57,980 --> 07:36:01,460
to a note in your ticketing system on the ticket

8912
07:36:01,460 --> 07:36:06,350
so you can sort of see it before you press send.

8913
07:36:06,350 --> 07:36:07,950
And finally, when you're confident,

8914
07:36:07,950 --> 07:36:11,110
then you can have an answer and actually respond

8915
07:36:11,110 --> 07:36:13,840
to the ticket and the user.

8916
07:36:13,840 --> 07:36:15,920
There's also things you can do around the guard rails

8917
07:36:15,920 --> 07:36:17,960
and environment you give Grokbot.

8918
07:36:17,960 --> 07:36:20,880
So you can set up evals and traces fairly easily

8919
07:36:20,880 --> 07:36:24,640
so you know what it's thinking and what actions it will take

8920
07:36:24,640 --> 07:36:26,280
before it publishes.

8921
07:36:26,280 --> 07:36:30,920
So when you can add traces to those draft steps

8922
07:36:30,920 --> 07:36:35,480
and then sort of make sure it's behaving the way you want it

8923
07:36:35,480 --> 07:36:39,620
to before going on.

8924
07:36:39,620 --> 07:36:40,120
Cool.

8925
07:36:40,120 --> 07:36:41,460
The second one.

8926
07:36:41,460 --> 07:36:44,420
So at scale, you may have a lot of support tickets

8927
07:36:44,420 --> 07:36:47,900
with a ton of different customer states.

8928
07:36:47,900 --> 07:36:51,460
And it can be really challenging to find the things

8929
07:36:51,460 --> 07:36:53,740
you should care about in that exact moment that

8930
07:36:53,740 --> 07:36:55,540
need your attention.

8931
07:36:55,540 --> 07:36:59,820
Grokbot can make this a lot easier through alerting.

8932
07:36:59,820 --> 07:37:03,100
So you can spin up a bot that looks for certain things.

8933
07:37:03,100 --> 07:37:07,510
For example, maybe your team is hyper-focused on churn

8934
07:37:07,510 --> 07:37:08,670
this quarter.

8935
07:37:08,670 --> 07:37:10,970
And you want to know when someone

8936
07:37:10,970 --> 07:37:13,590
writes in that is threatening to churn,

8937
07:37:13,590 --> 07:37:18,470
that has been a customer for six months or more.

8938
07:37:18,470 --> 07:37:21,190
Anyone on your team can spin up a Grokbot

8939
07:37:21,190 --> 07:37:24,320
that you have connected to your ticketing system.

8940
07:37:24,320 --> 07:37:27,420
You can just tell it to create a routine that

8941
07:37:27,420 --> 07:37:32,690
looks through your tickets every hour.

8942
07:37:32,690 --> 07:37:37,110
Try it classifies if a user is threatening to churn

8943
07:37:37,110 --> 07:37:40,370
and has been a customer for a certain period of time.

8944
07:37:40,370 --> 07:37:43,290
And then send you an alert in a Slack channel

8945
07:37:43,290 --> 07:37:48,320
so everyone has visibility.

8946
07:37:48,320 --> 07:37:54,560
And another one that maybe is more powerful than you think

8947
07:37:54,560 --> 07:37:57,520
or is harder to get to than you think

8948
07:37:57,520 --> 07:38:02,560
is an internal question and answer agent.

8949
07:38:02,560 --> 07:38:04,880
So if you've used other support agents,

8950
07:38:04,880 --> 07:38:08,580
they are primarily focused on customer replies.

8951
07:38:08,580 --> 07:38:12,020
It's surprisingly cumbersome to leverage the knowledge base.

8952
07:38:12,020 --> 07:38:15,580
You've already sort of created or accumulated

8953
07:38:15,580 --> 07:38:18,880
to answer your own teammates' questions.

8954
07:38:18,880 --> 07:38:21,240
You may have someone in GTM that is trying

8955
07:38:21,240 --> 07:38:25,470
to get up to speed on today's release about Cloud agents

8956
07:38:25,470 --> 07:38:29,770
so they can come prepared to a customer call or a TSE manager

8957
07:38:29,770 --> 07:38:33,330
looking for changes to the internal refund policy

8958
07:38:33,330 --> 07:38:39,560
over time and if that has impacted certain customers.

8959
07:38:39,560 --> 07:38:41,360
You have already done the hard work

8960
07:38:41,360 --> 07:38:44,560
of creating this knowledge base of both public info.

8961
07:38:44,560 --> 07:38:48,560
So think your Docs and Help Center as well as likely

8962
07:38:48,560 --> 07:38:49,520
some internal info.

8963
07:38:49,520 --> 07:38:52,160
So you might have suggested operating procedures

8964
07:38:52,160 --> 07:38:55,160
around how your teammates handle refunds

8965
07:38:55,160 --> 07:38:57,830
that is not public information.

8966
07:38:57,830 --> 07:38:59,870
You should be able to query this intelligently

8967
07:38:59,870 --> 07:39:04,180
within Slack, and Grokbot makes this really easy.

8968
07:39:04,180 --> 07:39:08,580
And maybe the last one and one of the cooler ones

8969
07:39:08,580 --> 07:39:10,340
is it can improve itself.

8970
07:39:10,340 --> 07:39:14,080
So finally, you can give Grokbot your traces

8971
07:39:14,080 --> 07:39:16,240
or have it look back on the last week of tickets

8972
07:39:16,240 --> 07:39:19,320
and sort of ask it to look and try

8973
07:39:19,320 --> 07:39:22,560
to find improvements for tickets that maybe could have been

8974
07:39:22,560 --> 07:39:25,600
handled better, could have been flagged earlier,

8975
07:39:25,600 --> 07:39:30,990
and it can go and do those things for you.

8976
07:39:30,990 --> 07:39:32,390
Cool.

