9241
07:55:37,660 --> 07:55:40,620
you can have your teammates ask the bot you've already set up

9242
07:55:40,620 --> 07:55:44,380
to answer customers and the knowledge base

9243
07:55:44,380 --> 07:55:46,460
to answer your own teammates.

9244
07:55:46,580 --> 07:55:51,010
So let's say I am someone on your billing team

9245
07:55:51,010 --> 07:55:57,880
and I want to know what is the refund SOP.

9246
07:55:57,880 --> 07:56:01,680
So this is I'm an internal user.

9247
07:56:01,680 --> 07:56:04,680
I'm asking a question.

9248
07:56:04,680 --> 07:56:09,160
And it should give me the internal knowledge for it.

9249
07:56:09,160 --> 07:56:12,620
So that's the most helpful.

9250
07:56:12,620 --> 07:56:14,260
So we sent it.

9251
07:56:14,260 --> 07:56:17,160
This may take about a minute.

9252
07:56:17,160 --> 07:56:22,990
But yeah, we should see us getting a response here

9253
07:56:22,990 --> 07:56:30,980
pretty soon.

9254
07:56:30,980 --> 07:56:34,980
So it's asking for permission to post in Slack.

9255
07:56:34,980 --> 07:56:45,360
Let's say always allow for this.

9256
07:56:45,360 --> 07:57:27,400
And it should reply here in a second.

9257
07:57:27,400 --> 07:57:46,200
Let's try that again.

9258
07:57:46,200 --> 07:57:52,060
Make sure it's running.

9259
07:57:52,060 --> 07:58:09,860
See the mess.

9260
07:58:09,860 --> 07:58:12,180
OK, it's going.

9261
07:58:12,180 --> 07:58:13,260
I ran this right before.

9262
07:58:13,260 --> 07:58:16,380
So you can see that this is what would happen.

9263
07:58:16,380 --> 07:58:18,100
It would reply.

9264
07:58:18,100 --> 07:58:22,850
And go from there.

9265
07:58:22,850 --> 07:58:24,170
Let's give it maybe 10 more seconds.

9266
07:58:24,170 --> 07:58:34,490
If not, we can go on.

9267
07:58:34,490 --> 07:58:36,240
Now it says it's replied.

9268
07:58:36,240 --> 07:58:36,740
Great.

9269
07:58:36,740 --> 07:58:40,010
So it added the emoji and it replied.

9270
07:58:40,010 --> 07:58:40,530
Awesome.

9271
07:58:40,530 --> 07:58:44,400
So that's one of the other use cases.

9272
07:58:44,400 --> 07:58:46,120
Cool.

9273
07:58:46,120 --> 07:58:49,320
So that wraps.

9274
07:58:49,320 --> 07:58:53,580
Again, cut the demo there.

9275
07:58:53,580 --> 07:58:54,780
So what we learned.

9276
07:58:54,780 --> 07:58:57,980
First, you can and should feel comfortable setting

9277
07:58:57,980 --> 07:58:59,140
guardrails for your bots.

9278
07:58:59,140 --> 07:59:01,540
You saw maybe at the end there it

9279
07:59:01,540 --> 07:59:03,580
was even more protective than I wanted to be.

9280
07:59:03,580 --> 07:59:07,060
But that's probably better for rights.

9281
07:59:07,060 --> 07:59:09,260
You can have them be read only to start, and then work up

9282
07:59:09,260 --> 07:59:11,700
to rights, and have manual approvals

9283
07:59:11,700 --> 07:59:13,380
for certain actions.

9284
07:59:13,380 --> 07:59:16,660
You can also scope permissions to your different bots.

9285
07:59:16,660 --> 07:59:19,540
So one bot might have permission to post automatically

9286
07:59:19,540 --> 07:59:20,040
to Slack.

9287
07:59:20,040 --> 07:59:21,460
One might not.

9288
07:59:21,460 --> 07:59:25,060
And make them approve those actions before they do them.

9289
07:59:25,060 --> 07:59:26,620
Second, start simple.

9290
07:59:26,620 --> 07:59:28,540
So the whole product is still so new.

9291
07:59:28,540 --> 07:59:32,380
I don't think there's one meta or one clear playbook.

9292
07:59:32,380 --> 07:59:34,940
And the product is meant to be very flexible.

9293
07:59:34,940 --> 07:59:38,140
So try to make the bots fit into how you work,

9294
07:59:38,140 --> 07:59:42,030
and not vice versa.

9295
07:59:42,030 --> 07:59:44,590
Third, try to get a little creative and experiment.

9296
07:59:44,590 --> 07:59:49,290
You can really have it build what is missing.

9297
07:59:49,290 --> 07:59:49,850
Cool.

9298
07:59:49,850 --> 07:59:52,360
Thank you so much.

9299
07:59:52,360 --> 07:59:54,720
That is the rest of the presentation.

9300
07:59:54,720 --> 07:59:55,960
And we can go into Q&A.

9301
08:00:00,500 --> 08:00:02,000
Oh, yeah.

9302
08:00:02,000 --> 08:00:06,220
So any questions?

9303
08:00:06,220 --> 08:00:09,190
Yes.

9304
08:00:09,190 --> 08:00:12,260
What's the price of life for this?

9305
08:00:12,260 --> 08:00:16,060
So it uses your usage that you would

9306
08:00:16,060 --> 08:00:18,340
have through any of your Grok subscriptions

9307
08:00:18,340 --> 08:00:21,220
or currently cursor subscriptions.

9308
08:00:21,220 --> 08:00:22,940
So it just tracks usage.

9309
08:00:22,940 --> 08:00:29,040
I'll say it can really depend on how involved your loops are.

9310
08:00:29,040 --> 08:00:33,640
So if you have classifiers that run before every ticket,

9311
08:00:34,320 --> 08:00:40,600
if you have it right traces and evals before doing that,

9312
08:00:40,600 --> 08:00:42,860
that can also change.

9313
08:00:42,860 --> 08:00:48,580
Right now, the way I use it, it's around $1 to $2

9314
08:00:48,580 --> 08:00:53,500
to answer more medium to complex tickets.

9315
08:00:53,500 --> 08:00:55,940
And there are ways.

9316
08:00:55,940 --> 08:00:58,060
I've already tried to experiment and found ways

9317
08:00:58,060 --> 08:00:59,460
to get that down a lot.

9318
08:00:59,460 --> 08:01:06,270
So there are a lot of maybe low complexity billing tickets

9319
08:01:06,270 --> 08:01:08,190
of a user just asking for a refund

9320
08:01:08,190 --> 08:01:13,880
or a user asking about a certain new email they received.

9321
08:01:13,880 --> 08:01:16,950
You can sort of bucket those.

9322
08:01:16,950 --> 08:01:18,950
You can run a script, have it run a script

9323
08:01:18,950 --> 08:01:23,470
to find those first or run that manually

9324
08:01:23,470 --> 08:01:28,030
so it has more of a contained sort of set.

9325
08:01:28,030 --> 08:01:30,150
And then have it reply all at once

9326
08:01:30,150 --> 08:01:32,350
with the script to those tickets.

9327
08:01:32,350 --> 08:01:35,030
And I found that I could get that down to about $0.20

9328
08:01:35,030 --> 08:01:37,510
a ticket for those low complexity tickets, which

9329
08:01:37,510 --> 08:01:41,110
is a huge sort of change.

9330
08:01:41,110 --> 08:01:44,030
If you've used any of the other sort of support agents,

9331
08:01:44,030 --> 08:01:46,790
you know that the cost per, they usually

9332
08:01:46,790 --> 08:01:48,030
charge per resolution.

9333
08:01:48,030 --> 08:01:53,150
And that's easily at minimum $1, if not between $1 to $10

9334
08:01:53,150 --> 08:01:54,110
order of magnitude.

9335
08:01:54,110 --> 08:01:56,950
And if you're having humans respond to those tickets,

9336
08:01:56,950 --> 08:02:00,790
that can be even noticeably higher.

9337
08:02:00,790 --> 08:02:04,230
And that's just like with half a day of trying to improve it.

9338
08:02:04,230 --> 08:02:09,720
I think there's a lot of gains and unlocks there especially.

9339
08:02:09,720 --> 08:02:18,490
Yeah.

9340
08:02:18,490 --> 08:02:19,210
You're good.

9341
08:02:19,210 --> 08:02:21,280
You're good.

9342
08:02:21,280 --> 08:02:27,170
Sorry.

9343
08:02:27,170 --> 08:02:29,290
How would you interrogate what's going on

9344
08:02:29,290 --> 08:02:31,610
if something goes wrong, especially with communication

9345
08:02:31,610 --> 08:02:34,610
in between multiple bots?

9346
08:02:34,610 --> 08:02:35,530
Communicate, yeah.

9347
08:02:35,530 --> 08:02:38,290
So one thing I didn't show in the demo

9348
08:02:38,290 --> 08:02:41,970
is I set up evals and traces for every run,

9349
08:02:41,970 --> 08:02:44,300
especially for the reply bot.

9350
08:02:44,300 --> 08:02:46,220
So this is pretty basic.

9351
08:02:46,220 --> 08:02:50,180
So all it is is you have a Postgres database,

9352
08:02:50,180 --> 08:02:54,740
tell it to create a table for a set of evals,

9353
08:02:54,740 --> 08:02:58,140
so things you want to test every single time you make a change.

9354
08:02:58,140 --> 08:03:00,380
And then another table for traces.

9355
08:03:00,380 --> 08:03:03,420
So every time reply runs, I ask it

9356
08:03:03,420 --> 08:03:06,060
how long it took, what files it looked at,

9357
08:03:06,060 --> 08:03:08,460
what files it ended up choosing to reply.

9358
08:03:08,540 --> 08:03:12,390
So usually it's like a filter down list.

9359
08:03:12,390 --> 08:03:15,990
And then from there, you can usually decipher where it went

9360
08:03:15,990 --> 08:03:19,110
wrong or things like that.

9361
08:03:19,110 --> 08:03:22,830
And that's something you set up in the reply bot configuration,

9362
08:03:22,830 --> 08:03:25,390
or is that like a meta bot that's

9363
08:03:25,390 --> 08:03:26,590
looking at the whole system?

9364
08:03:26,590 --> 08:03:32,620
So I set it up just with a connector with Superbase.

9365
08:03:32,620 --> 08:03:34,420
So I didn't show it in the demo,

9366
08:03:34,420 --> 08:03:38,100
but I just set up a connector to the build

9367
08:03:38,100 --> 08:03:42,420
bot with Superbase, told it it has access to Postgres now,

9368
08:03:42,420 --> 08:03:46,620
create traces and evals table, write to those tables

9369
08:03:46,620 --> 08:03:49,460
every time I ask you to run an eval, or create a new eval,

9370
08:03:49,460 --> 08:03:54,360
or run a trace, or answer a ticket,

9371
08:03:54,360 --> 08:03:57,520
even if it's internal, external,

9372
08:03:57,520 --> 08:03:58,760
even if it's a dry run ticket.

9373
08:03:58,760 --> 08:04:00,760
So even if it's just leaving a note on the plane ticket,

9374
08:04:00,760 --> 08:04:01,920
it's still writing a trace.

9375
08:04:01,920 --> 08:04:06,560
So then I can have a log of how it's thinking about it

9376
08:04:06,560 --> 08:04:07,800
and what it's looking at.

9377
08:04:08,400 --> 08:04:11,600
And then once you have that, you can also

9378
08:04:11,600 --> 08:04:14,600
have the self-improvement bot look at those

9379
08:04:14,600 --> 08:04:16,640
and try to improve the system.

9380
08:04:16,640 --> 08:04:18,500
I think that's a big unlock.

9381
08:04:18,500 --> 08:04:22,740
Awesome, thank you.

9382
08:04:22,740 --> 08:04:25,220
Yeah, and I was thinking about customer support

9383
08:04:25,220 --> 08:04:26,260
with phone calls.

9384
08:04:26,260 --> 08:04:28,100
I'm curious if you've seen someone

9385
08:04:28,100 --> 08:04:31,100
has set up Grokbot with a phone.

9386
08:04:31,100 --> 08:04:33,980
I'm curious if you've seen any interesting cases of that

9387
08:04:33,980 --> 08:04:36,620
yet, or if you've played with it at all.

9388
08:04:36,620 --> 08:04:37,780
I haven't done it too much.

9389
08:04:37,780 --> 08:04:40,460
I know Matt on the stream has set it up.

9390
08:04:41,260 --> 08:04:43,620
He has a good video on Twitter of giving it,

9391
08:04:43,620 --> 08:04:45,060
giving his Grokbot a phone number,

9392
08:04:45,060 --> 08:04:50,380
and I think trying to make a reservation with that.

9393
08:04:50,860 --> 08:04:53,140
I think that's something that's definitely

9394
08:04:54,300 --> 08:04:57,940
will be the case and want to explore.

9395
08:04:57,940 --> 08:05:00,980
So if you do it, let me know for sure.

9396
08:05:00,980 --> 08:05:10,630
I'll play around with it for sure, cool.

9397
08:05:10,630 --> 08:05:12,670
How do you guys think about implementing this

9398
08:05:12,670 --> 08:05:15,190
for non-technical folks in the enterprise?

9399
08:05:15,310 --> 08:05:18,350
I'm kind of concerned if a non-technical user

9400
08:05:18,350 --> 08:05:22,820
is writing stuff to Postgres or without guardrails on.

9401
08:05:22,820 --> 08:05:26,340
So the, sorry, I might have misheard the first part of it.

9402
08:05:26,340 --> 08:05:29,060
You're asking about non-technical users

9403
08:05:29,060 --> 08:05:30,580
and how they would plug into this,

9404
08:05:30,580 --> 08:05:31,420
and if there's any concerns.

9405
08:05:31,420 --> 08:05:33,740
Well, I guess because it's relatively easy

9406
08:05:33,740 --> 08:05:36,660
to spin up a bot to do whatever you need.

9407
08:05:36,660 --> 08:05:39,140
How do you put the guardrails so that they don't

9408
08:05:39,140 --> 08:05:41,900
mess up the production databases or anything like that?

9409
08:05:41,900 --> 08:05:43,860
Because it's relatively easy to be like,

9410
08:05:43,860 --> 08:05:47,100
oh, refund all the customers that are,

9411
08:05:48,220 --> 08:05:52,620
that should be refunded, but then they don't know

9412
08:05:52,620 --> 08:05:55,540
the technicalities of some of the backend stuff.

9413
08:05:55,540 --> 08:05:56,980
Yeah, that's a great question.

9414
08:05:56,980 --> 08:06:00,500
I know there's a lot of effort into making this

9415
08:06:00,500 --> 08:06:03,300
more multiplayer shortly.

9416
08:06:03,300 --> 08:06:06,180
Right now, I'd say whenever you first set it up

9417
08:06:06,180 --> 08:06:09,220
to set the guard, whoever's first setting it up

9418
08:06:09,220 --> 08:06:11,540
maybe can be slightly more technical,

9419
08:06:11,620 --> 08:06:13,860
or not even more technical, but just aware

9420
08:06:13,860 --> 08:06:16,460
of sort of like system architectures of like,

9421
08:06:16,460 --> 08:06:19,740
okay, if someone is gonna run this crockbot,

9422
08:06:19,740 --> 08:06:22,620
I'm going to give them a template,

9423
08:06:22,620 --> 08:06:25,860
and someone non-technical, I'll share a template

9424
08:06:25,860 --> 08:06:28,580
with them that says, oh, you can't

9425
08:06:28,580 --> 08:06:30,460
update the knowledge base by yourself.

9426
08:06:30,460 --> 08:06:33,890
One thing that I've tried that I like a lot is

9427
08:06:33,890 --> 08:06:37,010
using, I use Notion as the knowledge base

9428
08:06:37,010 --> 08:06:38,850
sort of just as like an easier example,

9429
08:06:38,850 --> 08:06:41,650
but you can use GitHub or Origin,

9430
08:06:42,130 --> 08:06:44,290
and that's really powerful because the primitives

9431
08:06:44,290 --> 08:06:48,540
of GitHub are like, you get PRs,

9432
08:06:48,540 --> 08:06:51,740
you get code owners, you get branches, all that stuff.

9433
08:06:51,740 --> 08:06:54,380
So instead of this knowledge base

9434
08:06:54,380 --> 08:06:56,340
where there's only sort of one,

9435
08:06:56,340 --> 08:06:58,820
you could give someone a template

9436
08:06:58,820 --> 08:07:02,300
on their self-improvement bot.

9437
08:07:03,220 --> 08:07:06,040
For them, you could say, oh, you have to create a PR,

9438
08:07:06,040 --> 08:07:08,380
it has to get reviewed by bug bot,

9439
08:07:08,380 --> 08:07:11,420
it has to ping me, I have to approve it,

9440
08:07:11,420 --> 08:07:16,520
and I can run the building blocks of like,

9441
08:07:16,600 --> 08:07:21,790
evals and traces, I can tell it to run the evals

9442
08:07:22,230 --> 08:07:25,350
against a specific PR branch instead of just main,

9443
08:07:25,350 --> 08:07:26,670
so I can have the output of that,

9444
08:07:26,670 --> 08:07:29,630
have some confidence that it's going to behave

9445
08:07:29,630 --> 08:07:32,350
how I want it to behave, and then make sure

9446
08:07:32,350 --> 08:07:34,610
it gets approved by the code owner

9447
08:07:34,610 --> 08:07:37,990
or the person that's most in charge or most technical,

9448
08:07:37,990 --> 08:07:41,870
and you'll have confidence then of,

9449
08:07:41,870 --> 08:07:44,230
they can still make updates, they can still be helpful,

9450
08:07:44,230 --> 08:07:47,380
but they're not sort of, you're not taking on

9451
08:07:47,380 --> 08:07:49,900
a ton of additional risk.

9452
08:07:49,900 --> 08:07:50,900
Awesome, thank you.

9453
08:07:50,900 --> 08:08:00,620
Yeah.

9454
08:08:00,620 --> 08:08:03,100
One question from Max, is it more token-efficient

9455
08:08:03,100 --> 08:08:06,100
to queue and bulk ask requests?

9456
08:08:06,100 --> 08:08:06,940
Sorry, what was that?

9457
08:08:06,940 --> 08:08:10,860
Is it more token-efficient to queue and bulk ask requests,

9458
08:08:10,860 --> 08:08:12,620
just bulking together?

9459
08:08:12,620 --> 08:08:13,740
Yeah, so it can be.

9460
08:08:13,740 --> 08:08:18,200
So instead of, I guess if you saw how I was talking to it,

9461
08:08:18,200 --> 08:08:20,660
I would just like tell it to reply to Alex,

9462
08:08:21,660 --> 08:08:26,420
that is pretty unclear, so I could have been more clear

9463
08:08:26,420 --> 08:08:29,020
by giving it a set of ticket IDs.

9464
08:08:29,020 --> 08:08:31,280
Whenever I tell it like that, it has to go look,

9465
08:08:31,280 --> 08:08:35,390
go find, maybe read, call the plain MCP,

9466
08:08:35,390 --> 08:08:37,750
get all of the open tickets, look through,

9467
08:08:37,750 --> 08:08:40,990
do a string search on Alex, try to find that.

9468
08:08:40,990 --> 08:08:45,670
That is a lot less token-efficient than giving it

9469
08:08:45,670 --> 08:08:54,100
more hard details that can look up easier.

9470
08:08:54,100 --> 08:08:56,200
I guess one last question for Max.

9471
08:08:56,200 --> 08:08:58,920
As Roshan, Matt, and Lauren have been creating

9472
08:08:58,920 --> 08:09:01,400
their company online, do you have any recommendations

9473
08:09:01,400 --> 08:09:04,760
on what to set up first for a customer support team

9474
08:09:04,760 --> 08:09:05,660
using RockBot?

9475
08:09:07,820 --> 08:09:09,460
Yeah, that's a great question.

9476
08:09:09,460 --> 08:09:12,700
I know maybe their sort of business has changed

9477
08:09:12,700 --> 08:09:16,180
a little bit, but I'd say usually there's about,

9478
08:09:16,180 --> 08:09:21,220
there's usually like an 80-20 of 20% of use cases

9479
08:09:21,220 --> 08:09:23,860
that create 80% of the ticket volume.

9480
08:09:23,860 --> 08:09:27,020
I would try to identify those and have something in place

9481
08:09:27,020 --> 08:09:29,860
and make sure those are in the knowledge base first

9482
08:09:29,860 --> 08:09:34,400
and have RockBot have really good knowledge

9483
08:09:34,400 --> 08:09:37,000
and SOPs there and run evals and test

9484
08:09:37,000 --> 08:09:39,760
and make sure everything it can handle, it is,

9485
08:09:39,760 --> 08:09:43,080
and then yeah, put RockBot in front of it

9486
08:09:43,080 --> 08:09:47,940
and try to answer, cool, amazing.

9487
08:09:47,940 --> 08:09:51,860
I think I am out of time and am going to send it back

9488
08:09:51,860 --> 08:09:52,700
to the studio.

9489
08:10:34,720 --> 08:10:36,030
Can you mic packets on?

9490
08:10:36,030 --> 08:10:41,780
Yeah, I can actually share my screen.

9491
08:10:41,780 --> 08:10:43,990
Awesome, welcome back everyone.

9492
08:10:43,990 --> 08:10:45,830
Hope everyone enjoyed that last talk.

9493
08:10:45,830 --> 08:10:47,950
We're winding up our day pretty soon here

9494
08:10:47,950 --> 08:10:49,030
and I think it's been a pretty fun one.

9495
